WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.930
When running an app, one may want to write
information to a file to have that information

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:10.190
ready to use later within the app or the next
time the app is run.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:14.460
An example related to gaming can involve saving
a player’s status or collection of items

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.880
used to play games.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:21.510
In our ongoing file example, we will examine
the code that writes to a file.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.720
We also want to see what happens if we try
to write to a file that does not exist and

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.070
what happens when attempting to write to a
file that does exist.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:35.520
To help demonstrate these situations, I have
my code editor, Visual Studio Code, and Explorer

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.620
side by side.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:43.710
I have the 314-write file open and the folder
containing the files we will write to in this

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.710
video.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.730
First, let’s look over this code.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:54.670
On line one, we have an open method to open
a file called 314-message.txt.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:01:03.949
The w indicates to open the file in write
mode.

00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:08.940
Like r for reading a file, w+ will open a
file for reading and writing.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:15.950
In looking at the file list in Explorer for
this folder, we do not see a file named 314-message.txt.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:22.460
In running this code, then, one of two things
will happen: either the file will be created,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.329
or we will get a message indicating that the
file does not exist.

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:32.890
Lines 2 and 3 write statements to the file
referred to on line 1.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.790
Write statements write text to a file.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:41.100
Then, line 4 closes the file to free up the
memory space the file occupies while it is

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.100
open.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:49.020
I will run the code, and we do not get an
error message, so that is a good first step.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:55.399
In our user folder, we also see the 314-message
text file appear.

00:01:55.399 --> 00:01:59.780
One key to managing files is knowing how a
programming language will react to attempting

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:05.829
to write to a file that does not exist because
knowing that determines whether we need to

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:10.129
write code to account for a file that does
not exist.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:13.660
For Python, such code is not necessary.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:18.310
Let’s open the text file that’s been generated.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:23.860
The write statements from lines 2 and 3 were
written to the file, but there is no line

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.709
between the two lines of code.

00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:32.240
As we fix this problem, we will also discover
how Python handles writing to existing files.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:38.180
Does the file get overwritten or appended,
meaning the text is added to an existing file?

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:47.450
First, we want to add an escape character,
a backslash, and an n at the end of the first

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.340
write statement on line 2.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:59.370
I will place the cursor after the word configuration
and type backslash n to tell Python to add

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.760
a new line at this point.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.670
I will then rerun the code.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.840
Then, I will open the 314-message text file.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:17.319
The text is now on two lines, and, and this
is important to know, the original file was

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:20.780
overwritten.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:26.900
Seeing this behavior begs this question: how
do we add text to an existing file?

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.080
We will cover how in the next video.

